1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,371
This is MATH 239, video 18 b. We solve questions 6 to 10 of the Practice Midterm. Video 18 a did questions 1 to 5.

2
00:00:11,821 --> 00:00:19,010
Question 6 is about binary strings: ambiguity, a generating series, and a condition on blocks.

3
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:24,772
Question 7 asks for one coefficient. It uses two binomial series.

4
00:00:25,222 --> 00:00:30,214
Question 8 is about the product of two series, and a proof by coefficients.

5
00:00:30,664 --> 00:00:34,931
Question 9 is about palindromes and a recursive expression.

6
00:00:35,381 --> 00:00:46,602
Question 10 is about recurrences. The announcement of October 22 says that recurrences are not on this midterm. The question is still good practice.

7
00:00:47,052 --> 00:00:50,508
For each question, pause the video and try it first.

8
00:00:50,958 --> 00:01:02,051
Question 6 a. Here is an expression for a set of binary strings. Is it ambiguous or unambiguous? We must prove the answer. Pause the video and try it.

9
00:01:02,501 --> 00:01:12,315
Read the expression in three parts. The first part, 000 star, makes a block of 0s. Its length is a multiple of 3.

10
00:01:12,765 --> 00:01:22,898
The middle part repeats a piece. Each piece starts with 111, then pairs 11. So it starts with a block of 1s of odd length, at least 3.

11
00:01:23,348 --> 00:01:27,209
Then the piece has 0s in groups of four, possibly none.

12
00:01:27,659 --> 00:01:38,838
The last part is epsilon, or 111 star. 111 star is 11 followed by 1 star. So it is a block of 1s of length at least 2.

13
00:01:39,288 --> 00:01:46,925
The middle part and the last part can both make a block of 1s. So one block of 1s can come from two places.

14
00:01:47,375 --> 00:01:52,602
An expression is ambiguous when some string is produced in two or more ways.

15
00:01:53,052 --> 00:01:55,356
Take the short string 111.

16
00:01:55,806 --> 00:02:07,689
Way 1. The first part gives epsilon. The middle part makes one pass: the piece 111, with no extra pairs and no 0s. The last part gives epsilon.

17
00:02:08,139 --> 00:02:17,525
Way 2. The first part gives epsilon. The middle part makes zero passes. The last part, 111 star, gives 111.

18
00:02:17,975 --> 00:02:25,911
These are two different productions of the same string. So the expression is ambiguous. One example is enough for the proof.

19
00:02:26,361 --> 00:02:33,999
Longer strings of 1s have more productions. Take 11111. Way 1 is one pass of length 5.

20
00:02:34,449 --> 00:02:38,801
Way 2 is one pass 111, then the end gives 11.

21
00:02:39,251 --> 00:02:49,149
Way 3 is zero passes, and the end gives all five 1s. So 11111 has three productions. A computer count agrees.

22
00:02:49,599 --> 00:02:57,706
Here is the written answer. Say ambiguous. Give one string, and show two different productions of it. This is the full proof.

23
00:02:58,156 --> 00:03:13,857
A common mistake: each part is unambiguous, so the student says the whole is unambiguous. This is not enough. By Lemma 3.9, each concatenation must also split in only one way. Here it does not.

24
00:03:14,307 --> 00:03:25,614
Question 6 b. We are told that this expression is unambiguous. Find the generating series of its strings, with length as the weight. Pause the video and try it.

25
00:03:26,064 --> 00:03:35,920
Piece A is 011, then 111 star. Its strings are one 0, then a block of 1s of length 2, 5, 8, and so on.

26
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:41,063
Each bit has weight 1. So the string 011 gives x cubed.

27
00:03:41,513 --> 00:03:48,063
Each 111 adds 3 bits. So 111 star gives one over one minus x cubed.

28
00:03:48,513 --> 00:03:53,206
Multiply. Piece A gives x cubed over one minus x cubed.

29
00:03:53,656 --> 00:04:03,469
Piece B is 000, then 0 star, then 1, then 1 star. So it is three or more 0s, then one or more 1s.

30
00:04:03,919 --> 00:04:10,085
000 gives x cubed. 0 star gives one over one minus x.

31
00:04:10,535 --> 00:04:14,716
1 gives x. 1 star gives one over one minus x.

32
00:04:15,166 --> 00:04:23,870
Multiply. x cubed times x is x to the 4. So piece B gives x to the 4 over the square of one minus x.

33
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:33,643
The end is epsilon, 0, or 00. They give 1, x and x squared. So the end gives 1 plus x plus x squared.

34
00:04:34,093 --> 00:04:37,698
Now combine the parts. The two pieces are on the left.

35
00:04:38,148 --> 00:04:44,847
A piece is of type A or of type B. By the Sum Lemma, one piece gives A of x plus B of x.

36
00:04:45,297 --> 00:04:57,435
The Sum Lemma needs a disjoint union. A piece of type A starts with exactly one 0, then a 1. A piece of type B starts with three 0s. So no string is in both.

37
00:04:57,885 --> 00:05:05,203
The pieces repeat with a star. By the String Lemma, the repetition gives one over one minus A minus B.

38
00:05:05,653 --> 00:05:11,967
Then the end follows. By the Product Lemma, multiply by 1 plus x plus x squared.

39
00:05:12,417 --> 00:05:26,945
So phi of x is a fraction. The top is 1 plus x plus x squared. The bottom is one, minus x cubed over one minus x cubed, minus x to the 4 over the square of one minus x.

40
00:05:27,395 --> 00:05:37,785
The expression is unambiguous, so by Theorem 3.13 this is the generating series. The question does not ask us to simplify it.

41
00:05:38,235 --> 00:05:44,400
Expand the series. The coefficients start 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 13.

42
00:05:44,850 --> 00:05:49,949
The coefficient of x to the 4 is 2. So length 4 must have 2 strings.

43
00:05:50,399 --> 00:05:55,049
The first is 0001. It is one piece of type B.

44
00:05:55,499 --> 00:06:00,427
The second is 0110. It is piece A, then the end 0.

45
00:06:00,877 --> 00:06:17,709
The coefficient of x to the 5 is 4. The 4 strings of length 5 are 00001, 00010, 00011, and 01100. The coefficients agree.

46
00:06:18,159 --> 00:06:27,034
A computer listed all strings up to length 12. Each one is produced at most once. The counts match the series for each length.

47
00:06:27,484 --> 00:06:40,689
Question 6 c. We need an unambiguous expression for the strings where each block of 1s of even length is followed by a block of 0s of length at least 3. Pause the video and try it.

48
00:06:41,139 --> 00:06:44,041
A block is a maximal run of equal bits.

49
00:06:44,491 --> 00:06:57,035
Test some strings. In 0110001, the block 11 is even. The next block is 000, of length 3. So the string is in the set.

50
00:06:57,485 --> 00:07:05,890
In 110, the block 11 is even, but the next 0 block has length 1. So 110 is not in the set.

51
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:15,641
In 0111, the only 1 block is 111. It is odd, so there is no condition. 0111 is in the set.

52
00:07:16,091 --> 00:07:23,771
In the string 11, the even block is at the end. No block of 0s follows it. So 11 is not in the set.

53
00:07:24,221 --> 00:07:36,637
Start from the block decomposition: 0 star, then 1 1 star 0 0 star, repeated, then 1 star. It produces each binary string once, block by block.

54
00:07:37,087 --> 00:07:42,677
Each pass of the middle part is a block of 1s, then the block of 0s after it.

55
00:07:43,127 --> 00:07:51,788
The notes write the same decomposition as 0 star, then 1 star 1 0 star 0, repeated, then 1 star.

56
00:07:52,238 --> 00:08:02,393
Split each pass by the length of its 1 block. An odd 1 block is 1, then 11 star. Any 0 block of length at least 1 can follow it.

57
00:08:02,843 --> 00:08:13,061
An even 1 block is 11, then 11 star. The 0 block after it must have length at least 3: 000, then 0 star.

58
00:08:13,511 --> 00:08:23,239
The last part, 1 star, is a block of 1s with nothing after it. So it must be empty or odd: epsilon, or 1 then 11 star.

59
00:08:23,689 --> 00:08:27,188
Put the parts together. This is the expression for the set.

60
00:08:27,638 --> 00:08:29,281
Here is the expression.

61
00:08:29,731 --> 00:08:37,603
The written answer has short explanations. Step 1. Start from the block decomposition. It is unambiguous.

62
00:08:38,053 --> 00:08:56,015
Step 2. Each pass is a 1 block and the 0 block after it. An odd 1 block can have any 0 block after it. An even 1 block needs a 0 block of length at least 3. The two cases are disjoint, because the parity of the 1 block differs.

63
00:08:56,465 --> 00:09:03,463
Step 3. The last block of 1s has no 0 block after it. So it must be odd, or not there.

64
00:09:03,913 --> 00:09:11,806
Step 4. We only restricted the parts of an unambiguous expression. So the new expression is unambiguous.

65
00:09:12,256 --> 00:09:24,523
An example. 011000101 splits into 0, then 11 with 000, then 1 with 0, then the last block 1.

66
00:09:24,973 --> 00:09:33,506
0001110 splits into 000, then the odd block 111 with 0.

67
00:09:33,956 --> 00:09:43,215
1100011 ends in an even block of 1s. So the expression does not produce it, and it is not in the set.

68
00:09:43,665 --> 00:09:48,891
A computer check of all strings up to length 11 agrees with the condition.

69
00:09:49,341 --> 00:10:02,931
Question 7. Find the coefficient of x to the 70 in x cubed over one minus 4 x to the 30, to the power 20, times one minus x, cubed. Pause the video and try it.

70
00:10:03,381 --> 00:10:10,421
Call the product of the two inverse powers F of x. Then the function is x cubed times F of x.

71
00:10:10,871 --> 00:10:19,745
Multiplying by x cubed moves every coefficient 3 places to the right. The coefficient f 0 moves to x cubed.

72
00:10:20,195 --> 00:10:30,713
So the coefficient of x to the 70 in x cubed F is the coefficient of x to the 70 minus 3 in F. That is x to the 67.

73
00:10:31,163 --> 00:10:44,987
Each factor of F is a binomial series. One over one minus y, to the power t, is the sum over j of j plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1, times y to the j.

74
00:10:45,437 --> 00:11:03,997
Expand the first factor with the binomial series. Here y is 4 x to the 30, and t is 20, so t minus 1 is 19. The term for j is j plus 19 choose 19, times 4 to the j, times x to the 30 j.

75
00:11:04,447 --> 00:11:15,476
Expand the second factor. Here y is x, and t is 3, so t minus 1 is 2. The term for m is m plus 2 choose 2, times x to the m.

76
00:11:15,926 --> 00:11:31,521
A term of x to the 67 pairs x to the 30 j from the first factor with x to the m from the second. So 30 j plus m equals 67. j equal to 0 gives m equal to 67.

77
00:11:31,971 --> 00:11:37,816
j equal to 1 gives m equal to 67 minus 30, which is 37.

78
00:11:38,266 --> 00:11:43,727
j equal to 2 gives m equal to 67 minus 60, which is 7.

79
00:11:44,177 --> 00:11:52,924
j equal to 3 needs 30 times 3, which is 90. This is more than 67. So there are exactly three terms.

80
00:11:53,374 --> 00:12:12,211
j equal to 0, so m is 67. The first factor gives 19 choose 19, times 4 to the 0, which is 1. The second gives 69 choose 2, which is 2346. The product is 2346.

81
00:12:12,661 --> 00:12:32,885
j equal to 1, so m is 37. The first factor gives 20 choose 19, times 4, which is 80. The second gives 39 choose 2, which is 741. The product is 80 times 741, which is 59280.

82
00:12:33,335 --> 00:12:56,461
j equal to 2, so m is 7. The first factor gives 21 choose 19, times 4 squared, which is 3360. The second gives 9 choose 2, which is 36. The product is 3360 times 36, which is 120960.

83
00:12:56,911 --> 00:13:07,471
The answer is the sum of the three products: 182586. A computer expansion of the series gives the same number.

84
00:13:07,921 --> 00:13:19,654
Common mistakes: forgetting the factor 4 to the j, reading x to the 70 and not x to the 67, and using the wrong top number in the binomial coefficient.

85
00:13:20,104 --> 00:13:34,461
Question 8. A of x is the sum of x to the n over n factorial. Its coefficients start 1, 1, one half, one sixth, one 24th. Pause the video and try all three parts.

86
00:13:34,911 --> 00:13:41,781
Part a. Put 2 x in place of x. 2 x to the n equals 2 to the n times x to the n.

87
00:13:42,231 --> 00:13:46,775
So the coefficient of x to the n is multiplied by 2 to the n.

88
00:13:47,225 --> 00:13:56,675
The row 1, 1, one half, one sixth becomes 1, 2, 2, four thirds. For example, one sixth times 2 cubed is four thirds.

89
00:13:57,125 --> 00:14:04,528
The answer to part a: the coefficient of x to the n in A of 2 x is 2 to the n over n factorial.

90
00:14:04,978 --> 00:14:16,093
Part b. Use the product formula. The coefficient of x to the n in A times B is the sum over k of a sub k times b sub n minus k.

91
00:14:16,543 --> 00:14:21,343
Draw the grid. Row i holds the term of degree i of the first factor.

92
00:14:21,793 --> 00:14:26,038
Column j holds the term of degree j of the second factor.

93
00:14:26,488 --> 00:14:42,339
A cell holds the product of the two coefficients, one over i factorial times j factorial. For example, row x squared over 2 and column x cubed over 6 give one half times one sixth, which is one twelfth.

94
00:14:42,789 --> 00:14:51,919
The cells with i plus j equal to n give the terms of x to the n. For n equal to 3, these are the 4 purple cells.

95
00:14:52,369 --> 00:15:05,148
Their sum is one sixth plus one half plus one half plus one sixth. That is four thirds, or 8 over 6. It equals 2 cubed over 3 factorial. This agrees with part a.

96
00:15:05,598 --> 00:15:21,086
In general, the coefficient of x to the n in A of x squared is the sum over k from 0 to n of one over k factorial times n minus k factorial. This is a finite sum, as the question asks.

97
00:15:21,536 --> 00:15:38,731
Part c. Prove that A of 2 x equals A of x squared. Compare the coefficients. Line 1: by part b, the coefficient of x to the n in A of x squared is the sum of one over k factorial times n minus k factorial.

98
00:15:39,181 --> 00:15:50,509
Line 2: multiply and divide by n factorial. Each term becomes n choose k. So we have one over n factorial, times the sum of n choose k.

99
00:15:50,959 --> 00:16:01,113
Line 3: the Binomial Theorem with x equal to 1 gives the sum of n choose k. It is 1 plus 1, to the n, which is 2 to the n.

100
00:16:01,563 --> 00:16:10,609
The picture shows the rows of binomial coefficients. Row 3 is 1, 3, 3, 1. Its sum is 8, which is 2 cubed.

101
00:16:11,059 --> 00:16:18,952
Line 4: by part a, 2 to the n over n factorial is the coefficient of x to the n in A of 2 x.

102
00:16:19,402 --> 00:16:29,642
This holds for every n in N. Two formal power series are equal when all their coefficients are equal. So A of 2 x equals A of x squared.

103
00:16:30,092 --> 00:16:47,607
Question 9. P is the set of palindromes: the strings that do not change when we reverse them. Find an unambiguous expression, the generating series by length, and a reason why the expression is unambiguous. Pause the video and try it.

104
00:16:48,057 --> 00:16:56,185
Take the palindrome 0110110. Its first bit equals its last bit. Both are 0.

105
00:16:56,635 --> 00:17:04,656
Remove the two end bits. The middle, 11011, is again a palindrome. Its end bits are both 1.

106
00:17:05,106 --> 00:17:11,719
Remove them. 101 remains. It is a palindrome too. Its end bits are both 1.

107
00:17:12,169 --> 00:17:19,529
Remove the ends again. The single bit 0 remains. A string of length 0 or 1 is a palindrome.

108
00:17:19,979 --> 00:17:27,915
This gives a recursive expression. P is epsilon, or 0, or 1, or 0 P 0, or 1 P 1.

109
00:17:28,365 --> 00:17:33,976
The notes use the same idea for the set B equal to epsilon or 0 B 1.

110
00:17:34,426 --> 00:17:38,351
Now show that each palindrome is produced exactly once.

111
00:17:38,801 --> 00:17:42,791
A string of length 0 is produced only by epsilon.

112
00:17:43,241 --> 00:17:47,785
A string of length 1 is produced only by 0 or by 1.

113
00:17:48,235 --> 00:17:58,965
A string of length at least 2 must come from 0 P 0 or 1 P 1. Its first bit equals its last bit, because f of s equals s.

114
00:17:59,415 --> 00:18:10,615
The first bit tells us which case. A string that starts with 0 can only come from 0 P 0. A string that starts with 1 can only come from 1 P 1.

115
00:18:11,065 --> 00:18:21,391
Then the middle is a shorter palindrome. By induction on the length, the middle is produced exactly once. So the whole string is produced exactly once.

116
00:18:21,841 --> 00:18:29,030
Also, each string that the expression produces is a palindrome. So the expression gives exactly P.

117
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:36,179
The expression is unambiguous. So the Sum and Product Lemmas turn each part of it into one term.

118
00:18:36,629 --> 00:18:44,010
Epsilon has length 0, so it gives 1. The strings 0 and 1 have length 1, so each gives x.

119
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:53,719
0 P 0 adds two bits to a palindrome. So it gives x squared times phi of x. 1 P 1 gives the same term.

120
00:18:54,169 --> 00:19:00,931
So phi of x equals 1, plus x, plus x, plus x squared phi, plus x squared phi.

121
00:19:01,381 --> 00:19:10,811
Move the two phi terms to the left side. Then collect: phi of x, times 1 minus 2 x squared, equals 1 plus 2 x.

122
00:19:11,261 --> 00:19:17,042
Divide: phi of x equals 1 plus 2 x, over 1 minus 2 x squared.

123
00:19:17,492 --> 00:19:21,993
Check. The coefficients are 1, 2, 2, 4, 4, 8, 8, 16.

124
00:19:22,443 --> 00:19:37,675
A palindrome is fixed by its first half. So there are 2 to the ceiling of n over 2 palindromes of length n. For n equal to 5, this is 2 cubed, which is 8. A computer count up to length 14 agrees.

125
00:19:38,125 --> 00:19:59,011
Question 10 a. Recurrences are not on this term's midterm, but this is good practice. Solve the recurrence a sub n minus 7 a sub n minus 1 plus 6 a sub n minus 2 equals 0, with a sub 0 equal to 5 and a sub 1 equal to 10. Pause the video and try it.

126
00:19:59,461 --> 00:20:14,714
By Theorem 4.8, the generating series is P of x over Q of x, with Q of x equal to 1 minus 7 x plus 6 x squared. The coefficients of Q are the coefficients of the recurrence.

127
00:20:15,164 --> 00:20:30,801
Find the inverse roots. The auxiliary polynomial is t squared minus 7 t plus 6. It factors as t minus 1 times t minus 6. Check: 1 plus 6 is 7, and 1 times 6 is 6.

128
00:20:31,251 --> 00:20:35,689
So the inverse roots are 1 and 6, each with multiplicity 1.

129
00:20:36,139 --> 00:20:42,368
By the Main Theorem, a sub n equals A times 1 to the n plus B times 6 to the n.

130
00:20:42,818 --> 00:21:02,658
For which n does this hold? Theorem 4.8 gives P of x equal to 5 plus 10 minus 35, times x. That is 5 minus 25 x. Its degree is 1, less than 2. So there is no polynomial part R, and the form holds for all n at least 0.

131
00:21:03,108 --> 00:21:12,687
Use the initial conditions. n equal to 0 gives A plus B equal to 5. n equal to 1 gives A plus 6 B equal to 10.

132
00:21:13,137 --> 00:21:20,753
Subtract the first equation from the second: 5 B equals 5. So B equals 1, and A equals 4.

133
00:21:21,203 --> 00:21:25,000
The answer is a sub n equals 4 plus 6 to the n.

134
00:21:25,450 --> 00:21:39,978
Check it with a table. The recurrence gives a sub n equals 7 a sub n minus 1, minus 6 a sub n minus 2. From 5 and 10, it gives 40, 220 and 1300.

135
00:21:40,428 --> 00:21:51,244
The formula gives the same numbers: 4 plus 1, 4 plus 6, 4 plus 36, and so on. A computer check agrees for the first 20 terms.

136
00:21:51,694 --> 00:22:10,617
You can also check with partial fractions. Put 4 over one minus x plus 1 over one minus 6 x over the common denominator. The top is 4 times one minus 6 x, plus one minus x. That is 5 minus 25 x. This is P over Q.

137
00:22:11,067 --> 00:22:22,224
A common mistake is a sign error in Q of x. Write the recurrence with all terms on the left side equal to 0. Then copy its coefficients into Q.

138
00:22:22,674 --> 00:22:29,842
Question 10 b. Here we go the other way: from a formula to a recurrence. Pause the video and try it.

139
00:22:30,292 --> 00:22:37,524
The term 3 to the n has a constant coefficient. So 3 is an inverse root with multiplicity 1.

140
00:22:37,974 --> 00:22:48,342
The term n minus 4 times minus 2 to the n has a polynomial of degree 1. So minus 2 is an inverse root with multiplicity 2.

141
00:22:48,792 --> 00:22:54,147
So Q of x is one minus 3 x, times the square of one plus 2 x.

142
00:22:54,597 --> 00:23:05,882
Expand Q. First, one plus 2 x, squared, is 1 plus 2 times 2 x plus 2 x squared. That is 1 plus 4 x plus 4 x squared.

143
00:23:06,332 --> 00:23:17,148
Now multiply by one minus 3 x on a product grid. The rows are 1 and minus 3 x. The columns are 1, 4 x and 4 x squared.

144
00:23:17,598 --> 00:23:22,334
Add the cells of the same degree. Degree 0 has only the cell 1.

145
00:23:22,784 --> 00:23:26,027
Degree 1: 4 x minus 3 x is x.

146
00:23:26,477 --> 00:23:31,789
Degree 2: 4 x squared minus 12 x squared is minus 8 x squared.

147
00:23:32,239 --> 00:23:40,687
Degree 3: minus 12 x cubed. So Q of x is 1 plus x minus 8 x squared minus 12 x cubed.

148
00:23:41,137 --> 00:23:51,718
To justify this, write the generating series of b sub n as a sum of three series. 3 to the n gives one over one minus 3 x.

149
00:23:52,168 --> 00:23:58,291
n times minus 2 to the n gives minus 2 x over the square of one plus 2 x.

150
00:23:58,741 --> 00:24:04,543
Minus 4 times minus 2 to the n gives minus 4 over one plus 2 x.

151
00:24:04,993 --> 00:24:10,241
Over the common denominator Q, the numerator P has degree at most 2.

152
00:24:10,691 --> 00:24:25,411
By Theorem 4.8, the coefficients of Q give the recurrence. b sub n plus b sub n minus 1, minus 8 b sub n minus 2, minus 12 b sub n minus 3, equals 0.

153
00:24:25,861 --> 00:24:33,883
The numerator has degree at most 2. So the recurrence holds for n at least 3, and we need three initial conditions.

154
00:24:34,333 --> 00:24:47,303
Use the formula. b sub 0 is 1 plus minus 4, which is minus 3. b sub 1 is 3 plus 6, which is 9. b sub 2 is 9 minus 8, which is 1.

155
00:24:47,753 --> 00:25:01,577
Check the row for n equal to 3. 35 plus 1, minus 8 times 9, minus 12 times minus 3. That is 36 minus 72 plus 36, which is 0.

156
00:25:02,027 --> 00:25:10,006
The rows for n equal to 4 and 5 also give 0. A computer check agrees up to n equal to 19.

157
00:25:10,456 --> 00:25:24,707
A common mistake is a recurrence with too few terms. The order must be the sum of the multiplicities, 1 plus 2, which is 3. A computer check shows that no recurrence of order 2 fits the values.

158
00:25:25,157 --> 00:25:32,901
Here is a summary. Question 6 a: to show that an expression is ambiguous, give one string with two productions.

159
00:25:33,351 --> 00:25:40,647
Question 6 b: for the series of an unambiguous expression, use the Sum, String and Product Lemmas.

160
00:25:41,097 --> 00:25:46,622
Question 6 c: for a condition on blocks, restrict the block decomposition.

161
00:25:47,072 --> 00:25:55,435
Question 7 used two binomial series and the index pairs with 30 j plus m equal to 67.

162
00:25:55,885 --> 00:26:01,773
Question 8 compared coefficients, with the product formula and the Binomial Theorem.

